Hi folks,

I realized recently that I don't quite get what stat_qq is doing...

My previous understanding was that if you say ggplot(df) +
stat_qq(aes(sample=colname), distribution=qwhatever), then you get
basically a geom_point plot where the y value of each point is given
by your input data and the x value is defined by the function
"qwhatever".

But I've been noticing that the y-values of the plotted points seem a
tad off...

As a simple example:

ggplot(data.frame(d=1:10)) + stat_qq(aes(sample=d),
distribution=qunif)

Ignoring the x-values for each point, I find it odd that the y-values
don't line up perfectly on the grid lines, and it's particularly
noticeable at the extremes.

I've been using this to generate plots of many thousands of points, so
I never really noticed because the overall shapes generally looked
fine to me, but when I started looking closely, I realized they didn't
match up with what I got from geom_point..

So is my understanding of stat_qq wrong, or is it a bug?

